IMO, since there are no reasonable people claiming to have a complete
model of any natural language, we shouldn't view any "rules", whether
they come from grammar books or recent linguistic theories as anything
other than the misconceptions and simplifications of their time.
Language is what is spoken by the language carriers. There are these
doctrines, *based mainly on misconceptions*, which require utilising
only a subset of language in certain contexts--and sometimes this
business gets silly enough for these enforcements to *change*
language, but that's another matter--even then, language always
changes in a fuzzy way.

